00:06:13.520 --> 00:06:15.500
So if you're interested
in this video course,

00:06:15.500 --> 00:06:17.790
check out the video
description below to the link

00:06:17.790 --> 00:06:19.120
to this mini course of mine.

00:06:19.120 --> 00:06:20.120
Next, commodities.

00:06:20.120 --> 00:06:22.050
So maybe you own some
physical commodities,

00:06:22.050 --> 00:06:23.670
for instance, gold or silver.

00:06:23.670 --> 00:06:25.590
Some gold coins or anything like that.

00:06:25.590 --> 00:06:26.920
Here again, you can track the value

00:06:26.920 --> 00:06:29.200
and then I would recommend
you to every half a year,

00:06:29.200 --> 00:06:31.820
or every year or so, just
look into the figures,

00:06:31.820 --> 00:06:34.130
update it according to
the current market value.

00:06:34.130 --> 00:06:36.990
Next, additional objects of value.

00:06:36.990 --> 00:06:39.170
So the classical example
here would be a car.

00:06:39.170 --> 00:06:41.440
Or maybe you also own an expensive watch

00:06:41.440 --> 00:06:44.220
or an expensive piece of
art or anything like that.

00:06:44.220 --> 00:06:46.800
This could be the place
where you list these objects.

00:06:46.800 --> 00:06:50.190
Here of course, be careful
that you do not fool yourself.

00:06:50.190 --> 00:06:52.800
So general or consumer
products that you own

00:06:52.800 --> 00:06:55.110
or expensive pieces of clothing,

00:06:55.110 --> 00:06:58.680
all of this I would not list
here, even if you might be able

00:06:58.680 --> 00:07:00.780
to get some money for
it if you would sell it.

00:07:00.780 --> 00:07:02.420
Often the actual market value

00:07:02.420 --> 00:07:05.210
of these objects would
be significantly smaller

00:07:05.210 --> 00:07:06.950
than what you might believe it is.

00:07:06.950 --> 00:07:07.830
So as a best practice,

00:07:07.830 --> 00:07:09.530
I would just not include these things.

00:07:09.530 --> 00:07:11.580
Treat them as a little
buffer that you have

00:07:11.580 --> 00:07:12.930
if things go really south.

00:07:12.930 --> 00:07:14.960
And only include things
where you really know

00:07:14.960 --> 00:07:17.010
that they will have a
significant market value.

00:07:17.010 --> 00:07:18.990
And this is of course
true for cars as well.

00:07:18.990 --> 00:07:20.600
The problem with cars is that the value

00:07:20.600 --> 00:07:22.370
will go down rapidly over time.

00:07:22.370 --> 00:07:24.610
So make sure that you
update the value of your car

00:07:24.610 --> 00:07:27.280
if you want to list it here at
all, every half a year or so,

00:07:27.280 --> 00:07:29.520
just to make sure that
you're not fooling yourself.

00:07:29.520 --> 00:07:32.510
So the next section in your
assets are your receivables.

00:07:32.510 --> 00:07:35.240
So again, some people might
not have anything like this.

00:07:35.240 --> 00:07:37.980
A receivable could for
instance be an expense claim.

00:07:37.980 --> 00:07:38.813
So what I mean by that,

00:07:38.813 --> 00:07:42.040
that when I was in
consulting, I was pretty lazy.

00:07:42.040 --> 00:07:44.230
And I only handed in my expenses

00:07:44.230 --> 00:07:46.390
every couple of months or so, which meant

00:07:46.390 --> 00:07:49.690
that my expense claims at the
time had at least four digits.

00:07:49.690 --> 00:07:51.240
Let's just put it this way, guys.

00:07:51.240 --> 00:07:53.010
So of course, this is
a claim that you have,

00:07:53.010 --> 00:07:54.750
this is a receivable that you have,

00:07:54.750 --> 00:07:56.730
and this might be something
that you want to list

00:07:56.730 --> 00:07:58.150
in such an overview as well.

00:07:58.150 --> 00:08:00.880
Of course, up to the moment
when then you get the money,

00:08:00.880 --> 00:08:03.400
as for instance here in
this example in April,

00:08:03.400 --> 00:08:05.390
well then the expense
claim would go to zero.

00:08:05.390 --> 00:08:07.300
But then of course you will have the cash

00:08:07.300 --> 00:08:10.280
on one of your accounts
listed here somewhere above.

00:08:10.280 --> 00:08:12.030
And then another
receivable might be a loan

00:08:12.030 --> 00:08:13.500
that you are giving to somebody else.

00:08:13.500 --> 00:08:16.260
So maybe you wanna help out a
family member or a colleague

00:08:16.260 --> 00:08:18.700
or a friend of yours, and you
decided to give them a loan.

00:08:18.700 --> 00:08:20.610
And then of course the value

00:08:20.610 --> 00:08:23.100
of the principle of that
loan would be an asset,

00:08:23.100 --> 00:08:24.820
a receivable for you as well.

00:08:24.820 --> 00:08:25.760
Of course, assuming

00:08:25.760 --> 00:08:27.700
that you can have the
reasonable expectation

00:08:27.700 --> 00:08:30.160
that this will be paid back
to you at some point in time.

00:08:30.160 --> 00:08:32.040
So this is the overview of the assets.

00:08:32.040 --> 00:08:33.910
Again, this can look very different

00:08:33.910 --> 00:08:35.400
for your specific situation.

00:08:35.400 --> 00:08:37.470
Of course, adapt this
to your specific needs,

00:08:37.470 --> 00:08:39.320
to the asset classes that you own,

00:08:39.320 --> 00:08:40.710
but this is the overall idea.

