00:09:23.460 --> 00:09:26.160
But now let's look into
the liabilities as well.

00:09:26.160 --> 00:09:27.990
For the liabilities, I
have three categories

00:09:27.990 --> 00:09:30.030
that now I want to talk about today.

00:09:30.030 --> 00:09:31.820
The first is the consumer debt.

00:09:31.820 --> 00:09:33.330
Probably the most common example

00:09:33.330 --> 00:09:36.420
of consumer debt is the open
balance on your credit cards.

00:09:36.420 --> 00:09:38.760
So at the time when you update
this every single month,

00:09:38.760 --> 00:09:40.210
just look into your credit cards

00:09:40.210 --> 00:09:41.430
and see what the open balance

00:09:41.430 --> 00:09:44.170
of your cards is at that
specific point in time.

00:09:44.170 --> 00:09:46.760
And then this open balance
that you still owe,

00:09:46.760 --> 00:09:48.420
this is the amount that
you should include here.

00:09:48.420 --> 00:09:50.800
Of course, if you have
any other consumer loans,

00:09:50.800 --> 00:09:53.560
any other consumer debt, this
is what you could include

00:09:53.560 --> 00:09:55.180
in additional lines here as well.

00:09:55.180 --> 00:09:56.190
Regarding consumer debt,

00:09:56.190 --> 00:09:57.693
of course, I trust I
do not need to tell you

00:09:57.693 --> 00:10:00.200
that this really should be avoided.

00:10:00.200 --> 00:10:02.660
If you just use a credit
card as a means of payment

00:10:02.660 --> 00:10:05.150
and then just pay off the balance
at the end of every month.

00:10:05.150 --> 00:10:07.200
But do not just start buying things

00:10:07.200 --> 00:10:10.010
on credit that you actually
cannot afford at the moment,

00:10:10.010 --> 00:10:12.910
just with the perspective of
hopefully paying for later

00:10:12.910 --> 00:10:13.980
at any point in time.

00:10:13.980 --> 00:10:15.380
Next is the real estate debt.

00:10:15.380 --> 00:10:16.600
So in this example where you

00:10:16.600 --> 00:10:18.460
own these two apartments in Dusseldorf,

00:10:18.460 --> 00:10:20.710
there might still be a
certain amount that you owe

00:10:20.710 --> 00:10:22.390
towards these apartments.

00:10:22.390 --> 00:10:23.610
And this is then of course,

00:10:23.610 --> 00:10:26.390
the debt that you have if
you financed it via loan.

00:10:26.390 --> 00:10:28.030
So yeah, make sure that
you track every month

00:10:28.030 --> 00:10:30.930
what the open balance is
on these loans, in order to

00:10:30.930 --> 00:10:33.460
correctly show the picture
here of your personal finances.

00:10:33.460 --> 00:10:34.520
Now another type of debt,

00:10:34.520 --> 00:10:35.990
that now I don't have in this example

00:10:35.990 --> 00:10:38.770
but that you could also add
are of course, student loans.

00:10:38.770 --> 00:10:39.960
So if you have student loans,

00:10:39.960 --> 00:10:42.440
which still have an open balance
of course, then list this

00:10:42.440 --> 00:10:45.427
as an additional line item
within your liabilities as well.

00:10:45.427 --> 00:10:46.900
And the last point I wanna talk

00:10:46.900 --> 00:10:49.740
about regarding liabilities
are the accrued taxes.

00:10:49.740 --> 00:10:51.510
So this would probably not be so relevant

00:10:51.510 --> 00:10:52.630
if you are an employee.

00:10:52.630 --> 00:10:54.620
But if you're self employed,
of course, you do not

00:10:54.620 --> 00:10:57.670
have an employer who
directly deducts income taxes

00:10:57.670 --> 00:11:00.860
from your paycheck, but you
often need to pay the income tax

00:11:00.860 --> 00:11:02.230
at the end of the year.

00:11:02.230 --> 00:11:04.060
And depending on the
size of your business,

00:11:04.060 --> 00:11:07.130
this can sum up to a
very significant amount.

00:11:07.130 --> 00:11:08.860
So if you're self employed, make sure

00:11:08.860 --> 00:11:10.100
that you have a good understanding

00:11:10.100 --> 00:11:13.070
of what your tax obligations
will be at the end of the year.

00:11:13.070 --> 00:11:14.680
And then of course, in such an overview,

00:11:14.680 --> 00:11:16.940
make sure that you also
track what the current

00:11:16.940 --> 00:11:19.600
sum of the tax obligations
in the current year is.

00:11:19.600 --> 00:11:21.580
Because of course, this
needs to be deducted

00:11:21.580 --> 00:11:22.830
to then get to your net worth.

00:11:22.830 --> 00:11:24.610
So that's it guys, this is how it works,

00:11:24.610 --> 00:11:27.550
this is how you calculate your
net worth to just add the sum

00:11:27.550 --> 00:11:29.870
of all your assets and
then subtract from it

00:11:29.870 --> 00:11:31.410
the sum of all your liabilities.

00:11:31.410 --> 00:11:33.120
But now let's dig a little bit deeper.

00:11:33.120 --> 00:11:36.110
Because indeed, to really get
your personal finances right,

00:11:36.110 --> 00:11:37.790
I believe it is crucial to also get

00:11:37.790 --> 00:11:40.120
a good understanding of your cashflow.

00:11:40.120 --> 00:11:42.730
And the way I look into this
is by looking into my income

00:11:42.730 --> 00:11:45.140
my recurrent payments and my investments.

00:11:45.140 --> 00:11:46.930
So what exactly does this mean?

00:11:46.930 --> 00:11:48.220
So first of all, under income,

00:11:48.220 --> 00:11:50.510
you have all the money
that you earn every month.

00:11:50.510 --> 00:11:53.060
So for most people, this
would be a full time job.

00:11:53.060 --> 00:11:55.980
Then you might also have some
income from a self employment.

00:11:55.980 --> 00:11:57.640
Maybe you have some other side gigs,

00:11:57.640 --> 00:11:59.530
some other side hustles that you have.

00:11:59.530 --> 00:12:01.200
You might for instance, be renting out

00:12:01.200 --> 00:12:03.000
a room in your home, at Airbnb.

00:12:03.000 --> 00:12:04.910
So the sum of all of this is your income.

00:12:04.910 --> 00:12:07.260
So what I do for instance,
for my self employment, is

00:12:07.260 --> 00:12:09.790
that I have a new
specific tab where I model

00:12:09.790 --> 00:12:13.060
all the income and the expenses
from my self employment.

00:12:13.060 --> 00:12:16.400
And then I take the profit and
then link it into this line.

00:12:16.400 --> 00:12:18.220
So for a self employment
like that, of course,

00:12:18.220 --> 00:12:19.680
there should only be the profit.

00:12:19.680 --> 00:12:21.690
But then of course, you
still have the problem

00:12:21.690 --> 00:12:23.900
with the taxes, because
usually then the profit

00:12:23.900 --> 00:12:27.070
from your self employment will
of course be a gross figure,

00:12:27.070 --> 00:12:29.030
so it will be a figure before taxes.

00:12:29.030 --> 00:12:31.960
And also on other income from
side hustles, for instance,

00:12:31.960 --> 00:12:34.480
Airbnb, you will often
need to pay taxes on that.

00:12:34.480 --> 00:12:35.410
So for these reasons,

00:12:35.410 --> 00:12:38.930
I here included a line that
roughly calculates the value

00:12:38.930 --> 00:12:41.180
of the taxes on this gross income.

00:12:41.180 --> 00:12:43.580
So here you should include
your marginal tax rates.

00:12:43.580 --> 00:12:45.810
So in my case, if you
already have a full-time job,

00:12:45.810 --> 00:12:48.060
this would easily be
around 40% in Germany.

00:12:48.060 --> 00:12:50.510
And then you deduct these
taxes from your income

00:12:50.510 --> 00:12:51.910
to get a better understanding

00:12:51.910 --> 00:12:55.137
of what your real after tax,
your net income really is.

00:12:55.137 --> 00:12:58.040
And also see how this links
together with the liabilities,

00:12:58.040 --> 00:13:00.560
with the accrued taxes,
because what I do here is

00:13:00.560 --> 00:13:03.050
that I pretty much just add up the value

00:13:03.050 --> 00:13:06.350
of all these taxes, up to the
current month in that year.

00:13:06.350 --> 00:13:08.260
And this is then also the way how I come

00:13:08.260 --> 00:13:09.657
up with these accrued taxes.

00:13:09.657 --> 00:13:11.470
Then I then add in this liability

00:13:11.470 --> 00:13:13.130
for the net worth calculation.

00:13:13.130 --> 00:13:14.910
So this is the income.

00:13:14.910 --> 00:13:16.410
So what's next that I have?

00:13:16.410 --> 00:13:20.110
Next, I have an overview of
all my recurring payments.

00:13:20.110 --> 00:13:20.943
And here of course,

00:13:20.943 --> 00:13:24.300
what exactly this is, might
differ significantly by person.

00:13:24.300 --> 00:13:25.740
Here, just a generic example,

00:13:25.740 --> 00:13:29.060
you might have some living
expenses, so rent, utilities,

00:13:29.060 --> 00:13:30.140
maybe some cleaning costs,

00:13:30.140 --> 00:13:31.980
Then you might have some
services that you use.

00:13:31.980 --> 00:13:33.510
So for instance, university fees,

00:13:33.510 --> 00:13:35.740
some fitness center memberships, Netflix,

00:13:35.740 --> 00:13:37.820
audible, some other
services that you might use.

00:13:37.820 --> 00:13:39.950
So what you see here, what I do is,

00:13:39.950 --> 00:13:43.360
that every payment, I
calculate as a monthly payment.

00:13:43.360 --> 00:13:47.200
So if it's charged yearly,
for instance, university fees,

00:13:47.200 --> 00:13:50.070
then I divide the figure
by 12, to understand really

00:13:50.070 --> 00:13:52.430
what the average costs each month are.

00:13:52.430 --> 00:13:54.880
And then I include
here, as an information,

00:13:54.880 --> 00:13:57.280
whether this is a yearly
or a monthly charge.

00:13:57.280 --> 00:14:00.540
And if it is a yearly charge,
in which month it is charged.

00:14:00.540 --> 00:14:03.070
Just for me to not get
confused and to know when

00:14:03.070 --> 00:14:04.800
these things will hit my bank account.

00:14:04.800 --> 00:14:05.880
Next insurances.

00:14:05.880 --> 00:14:07.860
So for instance, payments
for health insurance

00:14:07.860 --> 00:14:09.680
or liability insurance that you have.

00:14:09.680 --> 00:14:12.730
And then some other recurring
payments, for instance,

00:14:12.730 --> 00:14:16.230
mobile phone, banking account
fees or anything like that.

00:14:16.230 --> 00:14:18.450
So these recurring payments
are all the payments that

00:14:18.450 --> 00:14:21.510
for sure will be deducted from
my bank account every month,

00:14:21.510 --> 00:14:22.930
every quarter, every year.

00:14:22.930 --> 00:14:23.763
So these are the things

00:14:23.763 --> 00:14:26.440
that I cannot influence on a daily basis,

00:14:26.440 --> 00:14:29.100
But of course you can influence
this in the medium term.

00:14:29.100 --> 00:14:31.290
This for me already was a big revelation.

00:14:31.290 --> 00:14:33.650
When I put this together
for the first time,

00:14:33.650 --> 00:14:36.350
that I realized I had
all these little charges

00:14:36.350 --> 00:14:38.930
from some memberships,
some other subscriptions.

00:14:38.930 --> 00:14:40.820
I maybe signed up for
one or two years ago,

00:14:40.820 --> 00:14:43.070
which I wasn't really using at the time.

00:14:43.070 --> 00:14:45.590
So just transparency about
this is really crucial.

00:14:45.590 --> 00:14:47.470
And then always asking
yourself whether you're

00:14:47.470 --> 00:14:49.670
really still using this or whether

00:14:49.670 --> 00:14:51.300
you can delete or cancel this.

00:14:51.300 --> 00:14:52.960
So next section is the investment.

00:14:52.960 --> 00:14:54.750
So what are all the payments related

00:14:54.750 --> 00:14:57.940
to saving, related to investments
that you do every month?

00:14:57.940 --> 00:15:00.100
And in this example,
you might pay an annuity

00:15:00.100 --> 00:15:02.260
towards the real estate loan that you took

00:15:02.260 --> 00:15:04.140
to buy the two apartments in Dusseldorf.

00:15:04.140 --> 00:15:06.160
And then these would come
with some monthly payment

00:15:06.160 --> 00:15:08.280
that will hit your bank
account every single month.

00:15:08.280 --> 00:15:10.150
And then you also might
decide to participate

00:15:10.150 --> 00:15:12.570
in a stock investment
plan, where every month,

00:15:12.570 --> 00:15:14.320
a certain fixed amount is taken

00:15:14.320 --> 00:15:17.260
from your bank account
and automatically invested

00:15:17.260 --> 00:15:20.520
into certain ETFs or certain
funds that you've selected.

00:15:20.520 --> 00:15:22.770
This for sure, is how I manage my savings,

00:15:22.770 --> 00:15:24.570
how I manage my investments.

00:15:24.570 --> 00:15:28.000
I do not ask myself at
the end of every month

00:15:28.000 --> 00:15:31.300
how much money I have left,
what my possibilities are now

00:15:31.300 --> 00:15:32.830
in order to do some investments.

00:15:32.830 --> 00:15:34.430
But I have these fixed commitments

00:15:34.430 --> 00:15:36.670
that are directly deducted
from my bank account,

00:15:36.670 --> 00:15:38.860
directly after my salary comes in.

00:15:38.860 --> 00:15:41.030
So it's pretty much a
given that I will invest

00:15:41.030 --> 00:15:42.900
these amounts every single month.

00:15:42.900 --> 00:15:44.410
And then it's up to me to figure

00:15:44.410 --> 00:15:46.180
out how can I live with the rest.

00:15:46.180 --> 00:15:48.240
Because of course, now what this means is

00:15:48.240 --> 00:15:50.410
that the cash flow that you get here

00:15:50.410 --> 00:15:52.250
on top by taking the income

00:15:52.250 --> 00:15:54.340
then subtracting the recurring payments

00:15:54.340 --> 00:15:56.020
and subtracting the investments,

00:15:56.020 --> 00:15:58.090
this is all the money that is available

00:15:58.090 --> 00:16:02.310
to you for all your daily
discretionary purchases.

00:16:02.310 --> 00:16:04.500
So of course, this might
also be some important stuff

00:16:04.500 --> 00:16:06.270
like food or clothing

00:16:06.270 --> 00:16:09.010
or some other things that you
will need to pay every month

00:16:09.010 --> 00:16:10.930
but it's just not automatically deducted

00:16:10.930 --> 00:16:12.070
from the bank account.

00:16:12.070 --> 00:16:13.470
But then this can of course also be

00:16:13.470 --> 00:16:14.890
all these other fun activities,

00:16:14.890 --> 00:16:17.570
like going on vacation,
going to a restaurant,

00:16:17.570 --> 00:16:19.720
maybe having a great night
out with your friends.

00:16:19.720 --> 00:16:21.910
In the end, all of this needs to be paid

00:16:21.910 --> 00:16:23.910
via this free cash flow
that you have here.

00:16:23.910 --> 00:16:26.560
So what I started to do
in the beginning is to

00:16:26.560 --> 00:16:30.380
really track every month,
also all these expenses.

00:16:30.380 --> 00:16:33.950
So by going over all my card
expenses, by really tracking

00:16:33.950 --> 00:16:36.500
down every day, what
I spent the money for.

00:16:36.500 --> 00:16:38.870
But frankly, then it took
me so long at the end

00:16:38.870 --> 00:16:41.100
of every month to compile
this into such an overview.

00:16:41.100 --> 00:16:44.110
At least for me, this
was not worth the time.

00:16:44.110 --> 00:16:45.990
I'm happy with this
approach where now I just

00:16:45.990 --> 00:16:48.000
have this number that I know is available

00:16:48.000 --> 00:16:50.070
for me for all this discretionary spend.

00:16:50.070 --> 00:16:52.400
And then of course, what
I did to challenge myself,

00:16:52.400 --> 00:16:54.560
is that whenever this
number was increasing

00:16:54.560 --> 00:16:56.370
to a level where I felt
I do not really need to

00:16:56.370 --> 00:16:58.480
spend all this money
on discretionary stuff.

00:16:58.480 --> 00:17:01.580
Then I was increasing my
monthly savings commitments,

00:17:01.580 --> 00:17:03.170
I was increasing the amount

00:17:03.170 --> 00:17:06.070
of my monthly stock investment
plans, but then over time,

00:17:06.070 --> 00:17:07.700
of course you wanna have a share

00:17:07.700 --> 00:17:10.500
of your investments as high as possible,

00:17:10.500 --> 00:17:12.720
because this is what will influence

00:17:12.720 --> 00:17:15.650
what will drive your net
worth in the longterm.

00:17:15.650 --> 00:17:17.840
And then whatever is left,
this is then the amount

00:17:17.840 --> 00:17:19.290
that you could use to live your life.

00:17:19.290 --> 00:17:21.540
Of course, it's great to have a nice night

00:17:21.540 --> 00:17:23.200
out to go to a nice restaurant.

00:17:23.200 --> 00:17:24.960
So you need to find your personal balance.

00:17:24.960 --> 00:17:26.510
You can do all of these things

00:17:26.510 --> 00:17:29.290
to an extent that they satisfy
you, but also keep track

00:17:29.290 --> 00:17:32.050
of your financial goals, to
keep your savings rate higher

00:17:32.050 --> 00:17:34.360
and increase the net
worth of you over time.

